L000732	
L000733	To generalize from the Ising model and formulate a class of models with on-site symmetries, we find it extremely helpful to borrow the terminology of checks from error-correcting codes. Note that in codes they are usually parity-checks, but here we do not require them to measure parity of bitstrings.
L000734	
L000735	\begin{defn}[Checks and syndromes]\label{def:check}
L000736	A check is a function $C:\Omega \rightarrow \lbrace 0,1\rbrace$. We say $C$ is excited when $C(\bz)=1$. If $C(\bz)$ does not depend on $z_i$, we say $C$ does not act on $i$; otherwise $C$ acts on $i$. We choose a set of checks $\Lambda_{\rm C}$ such that each check acts on $\le g$ ($g$ is a constant) vertices in $\Lambda$, and each $i\in \Lambda$ is acted on by $\le g$ checks in $\Lambda_{\rm C}$.
L000737	
L000738	For each $\bz$, its syndrome $\bs\in \mathbb{Z}_2^{\Lambda_{\rm C}}$ is a bitstring collecting the values of every $C(\bz)$.   The set of allowed syndromes $\Omega_{\rm C}=\{\bs:\bz\in \Omega\}$ may not span the whole $\mathbb{Z}_2^{\Lambda_{\rm C}}$.
L000739	\end{defn}
L000740	
L000741	This last point is crucial for the Ising model, which have redundancies in the set of checks for the above Peierls condition to hold.  Indeed, we do not require a ``minimal" set of checks $\Lambda_{\rm C}$ nor would that be desirable in finite-dimensional models.
L000742	
L000743	\begin{rmk}
L000744	    In \cite{MBL_LDPC24}, a kind of Peierls condition with $L$ in \eqref{eq:Mz>Delta L} replaced by $N$, known as linear confinement, for low-density parity-check (LDPC) codes was used to establish a kind of many-body localization, where all low-energy eigenstates are stable under perturbations.  Actually, in this setting, it can be the case that $\Lambda_{\rm C}$ contains no redundant parity-checks, because LDPC codes are $k$-local models on expander graphs. In this paper we are most interested in systems where $L\ll N$, where the techniques of  \cite{MBL_LDPC24} do not directly apply.
L000745	\end{rmk}
L000746	
L000747	\begin{defn}[On-site symmetry and symmetric Hamiltonians]
L000748	
L000749	An on-site symmetry group $G$ is a set of invertible maps $\{\ell:\Omega\rightarrow \Omega\}$ that are on-site (i.e. $\lr{\ell\cdot \bz}_i\equiv \ell_i\cdot z_i$ is determined by $z_i$ alone, where $\ell_i$ could depend on site $i$) and form a group with multiplication being the composition, and identity element being the identity map. We assume $G$ is a finite group in this paper.\footnote{In all of the applications we will study in this paper, $G$ is a small group -- in fact $G=\mathbb{Z}_2$ in all applications we will study in detail, which are Ising models and spin-offs thereof.  It is straightforward to extend to other small on-site ``flavor symmetries".   However, all of the formalism that we are describing also applies to expander LDPC codes \cite{MBL_LDPC24}, where the finite group $G$ can be exponentially large in the number of degrees of freedom.}
L000750	
L000751	Each $\ell\in G$ and $A\subset \Lambda$ define a truncated on-site symmetry $\ell_A:\Omega \rightarrow \Omega$: \begin{equation}
L000752	    \ell_A\cdot z_i = \left\{\begin{array}{cc}
L000753	       \ell_i \cdot z_i,  & i\in A, \\
L000754	       z_i,  & i\notin A.
L000755	    \end{array}\right.
L000756	\end{equation}
L000757	
L000758	Hamiltonian $H_0$ is symmetric under this symmetry group $G$ if and only if \begin{equation}
L000759	    H_0(\bz) = H_0(\ell\cdot\bz),\quad \forall \ell \in G.
L000760	\end{equation}
L000761	\end{defn}
L000762	
L000763	
L000764	We define checks for symmetric Hamiltonians as symmetric few-body operators, from which the bottleneck structure can be constructed.
L000765	
L000766	\begin{defn}[Checks and bottleneck for symmetric Hamiltonians]\label{def:check_sym}
L000767	Given an on-site symmetry group $G$, we say $(\{W_k,\Phi_k,\mathcal{B}_k\}^K_{k=1},R)$ is a $G$-symmetric bottleneck structure, if it is a bottleneck structure in Definition \ref{def:peierls}, and furthermore the following holds: \begin{enumerate}
L000768	    \item For any $k,k'$ there exists $\ell\in G$ such that $W_k=\ell\cdot W_{k'}$, $\Phi_k=\ell\cdot \Phi_{k'}$.
L000769	    \item There exists a set $\Lambda_{\rm C}$ of checks that are symmetric: $C(\ell\cdot \bz)=C(\bz)\,\, (\forall \ell\in G,\bz\in \Omega)$, from which the bottleneck indicators are built on: Each $B\in \mathcal{B}_k$ is of the form $B(\bz)=C_1(\bz)\cdot C_2(\bz)\cdot ... \cdot C_{L_B}(\bz)$, checking whether some $L_B$ checks all return $1$.
L000770	\end{enumerate}
L000771	
L000772	\comment{
L000773	define the checks as follows: For each edge $(i,j)$ in graph $G$, and each non-identity $\ell\in G$, define check $C^\ell_{ij}$ that acts on $i,j$ and returns $1$ if and only if $z_j=\ell\cdot z_i$. Here we have implicitly chosen an orientation for each edge, because for the reversed orientation $C^{\ell}_{ji}=C^{-\ell}_{ij}$ does not yield new checks.
L000774	
L000775	For each connected subset $A\subset \Lambda$ with \begin{equation}\label{eq:dA<2L}
L000776	    |\partial A| \in [L,2L],
